    {
        "content": "<p>I'm trying to help diagnose the issue</p>",
        "id": 608856803,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1783469258
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I appreciate it! (<strong>edit:</strong> I misunderstood what <span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"933054\">Snir Broshi</span> was saying, deleted my previous two comments)</p>",
        "id": 608856934,
        "sender_full_name": "Matt Diamond",
        "timestamp": 1783469280
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Okay while I have no idea about the internals of <code>rw</code> and I'm treating <code>rw</code> as a black-box, I think this is the problem:<br>\nI'll denote the strange <code>1</code> as <code>!</code>. The goal contains <code>f (n + !)</code> and we're trying to rewrite with <code>f (n + 1) = f n + 1</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>When <code>f := n ↦ n</code>:<br>\n  Both <code>f (n + 1) = f n + 1</code> and <code>f (n + !) = f n + !</code> are defeq under reducible transparency.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>When <code>f</code> doesn't have a known value:<br>\n  Both <code>f (n + 1) = f n + 1</code> and <code>f (n + !) = f n + !</code> are <em>not</em> defeq under reducible transparency. We can't reduce since <code>f</code> is unknown.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>When <code>f := Nat.rec ...</code>:<br>\n<code>f (n + 1) = f n + 1</code> is defeq under reducible transparency, but <code>f (n + !) = f n + !</code> is <em>not</em> defeq under reducible transparency. This is because Lean's type theory (and thus the kernel) has some builtin rules for reducing recursors (such as <code>Nat.rec</code>), and <code>!</code> is not defeq to <code>Nat.succ Nat.zero</code> under reducible transparency but <code>1</code> is, so <code>f (n + !)</code> can't reduce but <code>f (n + 1)</code> can.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The conclusion I draw from these is that <code>rw</code> has special handling for defeq equations, and it can rewrite non-defeq with non-defeq or defeq with defeq, but it explodes if you try to rewrite non-defeq terms with a defeq proof.</p>",
        "id": 608860299,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1783470840
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>thanks for the writeup! that makes sense to me</p>",
        "id": 608860422,
        "sender_full_name": "Matt Diamond",
        "timestamp": 1783470953
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>But now we need someone who actually knows <code>rw</code> and can tell whether my analysis is correct, and also if this is a bug that can be fixed.</p>",
        "id": 608860454,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1783470970
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Very interesting! I investigated this a bit myself and found some weird behavior in how defEq reduces the left and right sides in the presence of the different underlying terms for the number one.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Add</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">One</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">succ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">succ_eq_add_one</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">succ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">succ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">succ</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">succ_eq_add_one</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- set_option pp.explicit true in</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">rec</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↦</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">have</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">10</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- helps us identify between `f n + 1` and this</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">suffices</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">succ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">trivial</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">succ_eq_add_one</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">specialize</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\">-- Uncommenting the line below makes `rw [hf]` and `with_reducible exact hf` inside the conv work.</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\">-- change f n &lt; f (n + 1)</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\">-- Now let's get rid of f to demonstrate that this is not about it, rather Nat.rec.</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\">-- Simply clearing the value of `f` turns it effectively into a variable and `rw` works because `f =?= f`.</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\">-- The more interesting case below is when `f` is unfolded.</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">unfold</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">at</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊢</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">clear</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"kn\">set_option</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">trace</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Meta</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">isDefEq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">true</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">conv</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">enter</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"c1\">-- rw [hf] -- This doesn't work. For some reason only the right hand side is reduced to `Nat.rec ... n + 1`. The defEq trace is:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"c1\">--</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"c1\">-- [Meta.isDefEq] ❌️ Nat.rec 0 (fun x n =&gt; n + 1) (n + 1) =?= Nat.rec 0 (fun x n =&gt; n + 1) (n + 1) ▼</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"c1\">--   [] ❌️ Nat.rec 0 (fun x n =&gt; n + 1) (n + 1) =?= Nat.rec 0 (fun x n =&gt; n + 1) n + 1 ▼</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"c1\">--     [] ❌️ @Nat.rec =?= @HAdd.hAdd</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"c1\">--</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"c1\">-- This suggests that defEq reduces the left and right hand sides with different reducibility settings...</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">tactic</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">with_reducible</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">exact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- This doesn't work. The reason is almost the same as in the above `rw [hf]`, but the left and right sides have swapped roles.</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\">-- The defEq trace shows:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\">--</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\">-- [Meta.isDefEq] ❌️ Nat.rec 0 (fun x n =&gt; n + 1) (n + 1) = Nat.rec 0 (fun x n =&gt; n + 1) n + 10 =?= Nat.rec 0 (fun x n =&gt; n + 1) (n + 1) = ?m.60 ▼</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\">-- [] ❌️ Nat.rec 0 (fun x n =&gt; n + 1) (n + 1) =?= Nat.rec 0 (fun x n =&gt; n + 1) (n + 1) ▼</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\">--   [] ❌️ Nat.rec 0 (fun x n =&gt; n + 1) n + 1 =?= Nat.rec 0 (fun x n =&gt; n + 1) (n + 1) ▼</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\">--     [] ❌️ @HAdd.hAdd =?= @Nat.rec</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\">--</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\">-- This time it's the left hand side that has been reduced to `Nat.rec ... n + 1` which is not defEq with `Nat.rec 0 (fun x n =&gt; n + 1) (n + 1)` under reducible transparency.</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\">-- exact hf -- This works because it reduces `Nat.rec ... (n + 1) =?= Nat.rec ... n + 1` on the left hand side, which matches the reduced RHS.</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I'm not an expert and would like to understand this better! It might be that I'm not seeing the whole picture in the trace and defEq is reducing because of the differences between the ones.</p>",
